            <ArticleTitle>Development of a brief questionnaire to predict long-term disability.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>1042-52</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1097/JOM.0b013e31817d361e</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="OBJECTIVE" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">To develop a brief worker-completed questionnaire for use soon after a work-related back injury to assess risk of long-term work disability.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">In this prospective cohort study, 1885 workers provided information about pain, function, psychosocial, and work characteristics about 3 weeks after filing claims for new back injuries. Classification and regression tree analysis was used to identify the best predictive model of work disability status 1 year after claim submission.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">Pain interference with ability to work (&gt;5, 0 to 10 scale), not currently working, and radiating leg pain comprised the best model which accurately classified 77% of the workers.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSIONS" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">A brief questionnaire assessing pain interference with work, current work status, and radiating leg pain might be useful in assessing risk of long-term work disability due to back injury.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
